# Arc Foundry — Screens, menus, and audio

This file fixes the screens Arc Foundry moves between, what each menu contains and where
each of its choices leads, and the audio cues the game plays. The status bar and the
build panel are in `specs/hud.md`, and the input that drives these screens is in
`specs/controls.md`.

## Presentation

Arc Foundry fixes no palette, no font, no menu layout, and no artwork.
`specs/overview.md` states the visual design and what a player must read at a glance;
everything else about the look is yours. One requirement is this file's own: every piece
of text a screen shows is legible against whatever sits behind it at the logical stage
size `STAGE_W x STAGE_H` (`1280 x 720`).

## Screens

The game is a state machine over eight screens. Its screen identifier is one of these
eight values.

| Screen | Identifier | What it is |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Title | `title` | The main menu, and where the game opens. |
| Map select | `mapselect` | The choice of one of the three maps. |
| Difficulty select | `difficultyselect` | The choice of one of the three difficulties. |
| How to play | `howto` | The rules, in a player's words. |
| Playing | `playing` | The live yard, in a build phase, a wave, or the finale. |
| Paused | `paused` | The pause menu, over a frozen yard. |
| Victory | `victory` | The run was won. |
| Overload | `overload` | The run was lost. |

### `title`

| Element | Constant | Content |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Title | `TITLE_TEXT` | `ARC FOUNDRY` |
| Tagline | `TAGLINE_TEXT` | `GROUND THE LOAD` |
| Menu | `TITLE_ITEMS` | `SALVAGE`, `HOW TO PLAY`, in that order |

The item at the current menu index is highlighted and drawn distinctly from the others.
`SALVAGE` leads to `mapselect` and `HOW TO PLAY` leads to `howto`. A dim slice of a yard
may show behind the menu.

### `mapselect`

Lists the three maps of `specs/yard.md`, The Substation, The Switchyard, and The
Transformer Yard, each with its name and a preview of its waypoint layout, and The
Transformer Yard's preview showing its two fixed housings. A `BACK` choice returns to
`title`. Choosing a map leads to `difficultyselect`.

### `difficultyselect`

Lists Easy, Medium, and Hard, each showing its wave count and how tough its Load grows,
as `specs/difficulty.md` states, before it is chosen. A `BACK` choice returns to
`mapselect`. Choosing a difficulty begins the run on the chosen map at that difficulty.

### `howto`

How to play, written in a player's words rather than as rules of a system. It covers:

- the goal, which is keeping the Load from reaching the collector;
- that a stamped rock rolls a random component type at a random quality where it lands,
  and that placing it is free;
- that each level harvests exactly one roll and every other rock hardens into an inert
  blocker;
- that committing the harvest is what starts the wave, and that there is no send control;
- that every rock and every component is a wall, so the yard is a maze the player builds,
  and that a placement which would seal a leg of the chain is refused;
- that the Load reaches each numbered waypoint in order, and that a flyer ignores the
  maze;
- that two matching components fold into one a tier higher, and that a recipe folds base
  components into a combination tower;
- that Charge buys refinement and combination-tower upgrades and nothing else;
- the controls, naming each key bound in `specs/controls.md`.

A `BACK` choice returns to `title`.

### `playing`

The live yard, its structures, the Load, the projectiles, and the effects, with the
status bar and the build panel of `specs/hud.md`. It covers the build phase, the wave,
and the finale alike. The game can be paused in place here: the simulation freezes, the
yard stays visible with no menu over it, and the status bar reads `PAUSED`.

The pause menu is opened from here, by `KeyP` and by `Escape`; `specs/controls.md`
states how each of the two resolves.

### `paused`

The pause menu, over a yard that is visible and frozen behind it. It offers `RESUME`,
`RESTART`, and `QUIT TO MENU`. `RESUME` returns to `playing` and clears any in-place
pause, `RESTART` begins a fresh run on the same map at the same difficulty, and
`QUIT TO MENU` returns to `title`.

`Escape` and `KeyP` each close the pause menu and resume the run, doing exactly what
`RESUME` does.

### `victory`

Shown after wave `N` is cleared and the finale of `specs/campaign.md` has run. It shows
the Maze Rating, the number of waves survived, which is `N`, and the Grid Integrity
remaining. It offers `PLAY AGAIN`, which replays the run on the same map at the same
difficulty, and `MENU`, which returns to `title`. The yard is not shown behind it, so
the status bar and the build panel of `specs/hud.md` are not drawn.

### `overload`

Shown the moment Grid Integrity reaches `0`. It shows the wave reached, and no Maze
Rating, because the finale is never reached from a defeat. It offers `TRY AGAIN` and
`MENU`, leading to the same places. The yard is not shown behind it, so the status bar
and the build panel of `specs/hud.md` are not drawn.

## Menu navigation

Every menu is fully operable with the pointer alone, with a touch contact alone, and with
the keyboard actions of `specs/controls.md` alone. The three reach the same entries, and
no menu needs more than one of them.

Where a menu draws its entries is this build's, so each entry occupies a rectangular hit
region the build lays out and reports through the `menuButtons` reading of
`specs/instrumentation.md`. That reported rectangle is the region the pointer and the
touch rules below act over.

| Input | Effect |
| --- | --- |
| The up action | Moves the highlight up one entry, wrapping from the first entry to the last. |
| The down action | Moves the highlight down one entry, wrapping from the last entry to the first. |
| The confirm action | Takes the highlighted entry. |
| The back action | Takes the entry that returns to the previous screen. |
| A pointer moved onto an entry's region | The highlight moves to that entry. |
| A pointer pressed and released inside one entry's region | The highlight moves to that entry, and that entry is taken. |
| A touch contact landing inside an entry's region, or traveling onto one | The highlight moves to that entry. |
| A touch contact landing and lifting inside one entry's region | The highlight moves to that entry, and that entry is taken. |

An entry is taken only when both edges of the gesture fall inside one entry's region: the
press and the release for a pointer, the landing and the lift for a touch contact. Two
edges in different regions take no entry, and an edge outside every region takes none.
Every menu that this file gives a `BACK` entry can also be left with the back action.

### What is highlighted on arrival

A menu reached other than by returning to it opens with its first entry highlighted, at
menu index `0`. Returning to a menu highlights the entry that led away from it.

| Returning to | From | Highlighted on arrival |
| --- | --- | --- |
| `title` | `howto` | `HOW TO PLAY` |
| `title` | `mapselect` | `SALVAGE` |
| `title` | `QUIT TO MENU` on `paused`, or `MENU` on `victory` or `overload` | `SALVAGE` |
| `mapselect` | `difficultyselect` | The map entry that was chosen |

## Audio

{{#if (eq engine.slug "none")}}
The audio layer is part of the runtime you write. Define and play exactly the twelve cues
in `CUES`, under exactly these names, one per event:
{{else if (eq engine.slug "simple-2d")}}
Audio plays through the engine's cue bus. Define exactly the twelve cues in `CUES` from
`initialize`, binding each to the file `specs/assets.md` produces for it, and play them
by name from `update`, under exactly these names, one per event:
{{else}}
Audio plays through the engine's cue bus. Define exactly the twelve cues in `CUES` from
the game instance's `initialize`, binding each to the file `specs/assets.md` produces for
it, and play them by name from the world's audio inside a tick, under exactly these
names, one per event:
{{/if}}

| Cue | Constant | Played when |
| --- | --- | --- |
| `stamp` | `CUES.stamp` | A rock lands and rolls. |
| `fire-bolt` | `CUES.fireBolt` | A Capacitor, a Choke, or a Rectifier fires. |
| `fire-spark` | `CUES.fireSpark` | An Emitter fires. |
| `fire-chain` | `CUES.fireChain` | A Coil fires. |
| `fire-discharge` | `CUES.fireDischarge` | An Arc-Node or a Discharge Rig fires. |
| `combine` | `CUES.combine` | A combine of either kind resolves. |
| `kill` | `CUES.kill` | A unit dies. |
| `leak` | `CUES.leak` | A unit grounds out at the collector. |
| `slow` | `CUES.slow` | A slow is applied to a unit. |
| `burn` | `CUES.burn` | A burn is applied to a unit. |
| `settle` | `CUES.settle` | Unharvested candidates harden into blockers at wave start. |
| `music` | `CUES.music` | Looped under the yard from the first build phase onward. |

A combination tower's shot plays the cue of the family its dominant output belongs to.
Each of the eleven effect cues is a distinct sound, so the events are told apart by ear.

{{#if (eq engine.slug "none")}}
Each cue is played on the update its event happens, and at most once on that update; an
update that raises the same event several times plays its cue once. The music cue loops
until the game ends. Muting and the first-interaction unlock belong to the runtime layer
you write: the game binds the mute action to that layer's mute bit, toggles it from any
screen, and stays fully playable with sound muted.
{{else}}
Each cue is played on the frame its event happens, by the code that raised it, and at
most once on that frame; a frame that raises the same event several times plays its cue
once. The music cue loops until the game ends. Muting and the first-interaction unlock
belong to the engine: the game binds the mute action to the engine's mute bit, toggles it
from any screen, and stays fully playable with sound muted.
{{/if}}

## Out of scope

- Network play, and any progress persisted between sessions.
- Gamepad input.
- A map editor or generated maps. The three maps of `specs/yard.md` are the whole set.
- Any Load type, component type, quality tier, combination tower, or mechanic beyond
  those this specification states.
